WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:03.180
YUFENG GUO: Welcome to
part 2 of our episode

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.140
on using AutoML Vision.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.910
If you missed part
1, you'll definitely

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:08.080
want to watch that first.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.350
I've included a link to that
in the description below.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:14.800
OK, so in part 1, we talked
about what AutoML Vision

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.640
is used for and what
the data it's expecting

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.220
should look like.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.480
We also gathered and
prepared our training data.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.710
Now let's see how to use
our data to train our model.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:30.220
That's just as simple
as clicking Train.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.890
I'd recommend starting with the
simple model first of the two

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.260
choices and seeing
how it performs.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.530
After training is started,
go for a walk, grab a coffee.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.800
It'll take a little bit of time.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:42.970
And we're back.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.530
Once training
completes, you'll get

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.690
all sorts of statistics
about your model

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.970
which you can use to
see how it performed

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:50.920
and whether there were
some [? images ?] that

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.970
were perhaps mislabeled or
other aspects worth correcting.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.570
And then you can actually click
Retrain to fix those things.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:02.020
In our case, since we gathered
very specific and clean data

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:06.080
by design, we got some
astoundingly high metrics.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.890
However, what
really counts is how

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.670
this model performs on new
previously never before seen

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.320
data.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.990
I took some pictures to
try to challenge the model,

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.090
see what it would return.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:20.350
Let's try this picture.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:25.010
It contains the bike along
with yellow and blue chairs.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:25.510
OK.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.240
So that image was recognized
as primarily the bike

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.820
but it's got a little bit of
yellow and blue chair in it

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:31.632
as well.

00:01:31.632 --> 00:01:34.090
They're in the background and
far less prominently featured

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.470
in this photo, so
that makes sense.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.420
Let's try another.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.390
This picture is
mostly yellow chairs

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:42.790
but there's a couple of
blue chairs in there,

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:46.570
and the model thinks it's
seeing mostly yellow chairs

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.760
with a bit of blue chair.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.750
And how about this photo
of mostly blue chairs?

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.150
Yep, that's mostly blue chairs,
and it's got a splash of table.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.730
And interestingly, a
bit of yellow chair,

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.750
which is not expected.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.620
So not everything is going to be
perfect with a machine learning

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:06.700
model, but so far,
this model's top option

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.160
has proven to be quite good.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.440
Now finally, let's take
a look at this picture.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.320
Very similar to the previous
one but the front chair

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.270
is now a yellow chair.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.236
What will the model
think of that?

00:02:18.236 --> 00:02:18.986
Wow, look at that.

00:02:18.986 --> 00:02:20.402
The yellow chair
in the foreground

00:02:20.402 --> 00:02:21.940
really won out big time.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.990
It got a way higher score.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:24.490
All right.

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:28.100
Enough playing around with
those pictures for now.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.790
Note that this model is
already available at this stage

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:32.376
to be called via its REST API.

00:02:32.376 --> 00:02:34.000
All we did was train
it but it actually

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.770
deployed it for us as well.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:39.100
The service leverages Cloud
ML Engine's online prediction

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.040
capabilities to provide
an auto-scaling prediction

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:46.840
service that serves a customized
model, trained on our data set.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.730
The neat part about
all of this is

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.550
that once you have your
data pipeline worked out

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:55.240
from raw data to
well-formed CSVs,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.550
the process of training and
deploying machine learning

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:01.090
models is completely hands-free
in that you don't have to write

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:03.506
any code-- just click and wait.

00:03:03.506 --> 00:03:05.380
This allows you to focus
on getting your data

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.500
in a good state and,
hands-off, the challenges

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.410
of constructing a suitable
computer vision machine

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:14.890
learning model to the service.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:16.990
Thanks for watching
this episode of Cloud AI

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:20.200
Adventures, and if you enjoyed
it, hit that Like button

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.840
down below and be
sure to subscribe

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.890
to get all the latest updates
right when they come out.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.890
Now I'm going to go and
find me some more colorful

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:33.480
chairs to take videos of
so I can expand my data set

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.270
for my AutoML model.

